WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:12.162
Hi! Hot soup cools down if you leave it, and ice cream melts in your hand. Both are about heat. Today let's learn about heat and temperature.

2
00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:18.906
The Sun, a fire, a stove and an electric heater all give out heat. They are heat sources.

3
00:00:19.056 --> 00:00:25.074
Heat is a form of energy. It can make things warmer, melt them or make them boil.

4
00:00:25.224 --> 00:00:31.746
Temperature tells us how hot something is. We measure it with a thermometer, in degrees Celsius.

5
00:00:31.896 --> 00:00:41.178
Be careful: heat is energy, while temperature is how hot something is. A thermometer measures temperature, not heat.

6
00:00:41.328 --> 00:00:46.722
Put a cup of water at 80 degrees into a basin of water at 20 degrees.

7
00:00:46.872 --> 00:00:57.474
Heat flows from the hotter water to the colder water. The hot water loses heat and its temperature falls. The cold water gains heat and its temperature rises.

8
00:00:57.624 --> 00:01:04.602
This goes on until both reach the same temperature. Then the heat stops flowing.

9
00:01:04.752 --> 00:01:15.762
Things expand when they gain heat and contract when they lose heat. Put a dented ping-pong ball in hot water: the air inside gains heat and expands, and the dent pops out.

10
00:01:15.912 --> 00:01:26.850
Ice gains heat and melts into water; water gains heat, boils and turns into water vapour. Water that loses heat freezes into ice.

11
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:34.842
Metals let heat pass through quickly. They are good conductors of heat, so pots are made of metal to cook food quickly.

12
00:01:34.992 --> 00:01:45.666
Wood, plastic, rubber and air let heat pass through slowly. They are poor conductors, so pot handles are made of plastic or wood to keep our hands safe.

13
00:01:45.816 --> 00:01:52.722
Watch out! When you hold ice, your hand feels cold, but it's not because cold goes into your hand!

14
00:01:52.872 --> 00:02:01.818
In fact heat flows from your warmer hand to the colder ice. Your hand loses heat, so it feels cold, and the ice gains heat and melts.

15
00:02:01.968 --> 00:02:07.722
Remember: only heat flows, and always from hotter to colder.

16
00:02:07.872 --> 00:02:19.146
Your turn! A cup of hot tea is left on the table for a long time. What happens in the end? Pause and think.

17
00:02:19.296 --> 00:02:28.674
The tea loses heat to the cooler air around it, and its temperature falls until it is the same as the surroundings. Did you get it right?

18
00:02:28.824 --> 00:02:37.650
Last one! Why are pot handles often made of plastic? Pause and think.

19
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:48.402
Because plastic is a poor conductor of heat. Heat passes through it slowly, so the handle doesn't get too hot to hold. Did you get it right?

20
00:02:48.552 --> 00:02:56.994
Remember: heat is energy, and temperature is how hot something is. Heat flows from hotter to colder until both are the same.

21
00:02:57.144 --> 00:03:08.322
Gaining heat makes things expand or melt, and losing heat makes them contract or freeze. Metals are good conductors; wood, plastic, rubber and air are poor ones.

22
00:03:08.472 --> 00:03:13.962
Next time you have hot soup, think about where its heat goes!

